    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d149">

                <pb facs="d149-01" n="1" xml:id="d149-01"/>

                <head>149. Letter from President Nixon to Chinese Premier Chou En-lai, Washington, June 28, 1972<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d149-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d149-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1972-06-28"
                        >June 28, 1972</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>June 28, 1972</p>
                <p>Dear Mr. Premier -</p>
                <p>I wish to take this opportunity to express my appreciation for the courtesies you
                    extended to Dr. Kissigner on his recent visit to Peking. He reported to me at
                    great length on this talks with you and I was pleased to note that the candor
                    and the constructive attitude which have characterized our discussion in the
                    past are continuing at this time.</p>
                <p>I trust we can continue to work together toward the goal of normalizing relations
                    between our two countries.</p>
                <pb facs="d149-02" n="2" xml:id="d149-02"/>
                <p>Mrs. Nixon and I have the most pleasant
                    memories of our visit to Peking.</p>
                <p>She joins me in extending our warmest personal regards to Madam Chou and to you.</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        Richard Nixon
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d149-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d149-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Library of Congress, Manuscript Division, Kissinger Papers, Geopolitical Files, China, Chron, May-June
                    1972. No classification marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d149-f2" n="2" xml:id="d149-f2-content">
                <p>
                    Nixon thanked Chou En-lai for receiving his Assistant for
                    National Security Affairs Kissinger and
                    promised to continue the work of normalization.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
